WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.320
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.640
You know, I think many people want to
learn a number of things, including

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.290
they want to learn languages.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.920
I think a lot of people
don't know how to learn.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.940
So my video today is about
learning how to learn.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.510
And remember, if you enjoy
these videos, please subscribe.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.970
You can click on the bell
and you'll get notifications.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.950
Now, what I'm about to
say is very much personal.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:32.070
How I view learning based on my
experience, watching others, what

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:37.320
works for me and the reading that I
have done about how the brain learns.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.970
And I'm not a neuroscientist.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:47.730
I often quote Manfred Spitzer
Lernen...you know, brain

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.140
research and the school of life.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:55.305
And I just looked into my library,
you know, uh, The Mind and the Brain,

00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:59.205
which talks about how we can will
our brain to do certain things.

00:00:59.925 --> 00:01:00.644
The Right Mind.

00:01:00.705 --> 00:01:02.625
I mean, there's lots of
books I've read on the brain.

00:01:03.615 --> 00:01:08.655
My view is that to be successful
at learning, in other words, to

00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:11.655
learn how to learn, you have to have

00:01:12.065 --> 00:01:17.465
uh, an image, a vision of how
you think your brain learns.

00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:24.755
Um, my vision of how my brain learns
is based on my experience and what

00:01:24.755 --> 00:01:27.125
I've read on how the brain learns.

00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:33.515
So I'm persuaded that our brain,
and Manfred Spitzer refers to this

00:01:33.515 --> 00:01:39.215
quite often, our brain starts to
form neural connections between...

00:01:39.215 --> 00:01:40.775
connections between neurons.

00:01:41.535 --> 00:01:43.755
Uh, based on our experience.

00:01:44.505 --> 00:01:49.395
And as long as the brain is
gaining new experience, it's

00:01:49.395 --> 00:01:51.554
forming new neural connections.

00:01:52.184 --> 00:01:57.015
So it's constantly learning,  the
brain cannot do otherwise than learn.

00:01:58.095 --> 00:02:06.255
Um, however, Spitzer points out the brain
is not very good at learning details

00:02:06.554 --> 00:02:08.505
because whatever it learns, it forgets.

00:02:09.044 --> 00:02:13.605
So that the specific things that we are
trying to learn, which might be a list

00:02:13.605 --> 00:02:17.295
of vocabulary items, it might be grammar
rules, it might be something else...

00:02:17.715 --> 00:02:20.685
those things, we can retain
them for a short time and then

00:02:20.685 --> 00:02:21.674
they're quickly forgotten.

00:02:22.304 --> 00:02:28.755
But the underlying patterns in those
bits of information is what the brain

00:02:28.755 --> 00:02:30.915
is very good at putting together.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.105
And that's why the...

00:02:33.105 --> 00:02:36.644
if we weren't able to do that, we
would be dealing with every situation

00:02:36.644 --> 00:02:41.834
in life as if it was the first time
we ever encountered that situation.

00:02:41.864 --> 00:02:48.554
Whereas in fact, what happens is the
brain builds up these patterns to enable

00:02:48.554 --> 00:02:50.535
us to navigate our way through life.

00:02:50.565 --> 00:02:53.625
That's true of everything
that happens to us in life.

00:02:53.655 --> 00:02:55.515
It's true in language learning.

00:02:55.905 --> 00:03:01.214
So my focus in language learning, as
I've said many times, is to expose

00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:07.364
myself to as much language, if I'm
learning a language, language, content

00:03:07.394 --> 00:03:14.070
as possible and not to worry too much
about specific rules, specific words.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.090
Uh, you know, I saw a thing the
other day, teachers on Twitter were

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.700
saying, you know, should we teach kids

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.890
these words or those words?

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.270
You know, two different word lists.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.760
It's not up to the teacher to decide
which words the kids should learn

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.800
or to attempt to teach these words.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:38.460
All the teacher can do is to
provide an environment where the

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:42.480
learner is motivated, is interested
because it's something of interest.

00:03:43.079 --> 00:03:48.090
And by being exposed to this over
and over again, gradually certain

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:51.120
patterns will form in the brain.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:55.920
And so, you know, I'm
quite content in my Arabic.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.170
I'm not ever going to test myself.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.675
Uh, I'm just happy to listen to, uh,
you know, a transcript of an, of a

00:04:03.675 --> 00:04:09.585
podcast, uh, go through it, maybe
in sentence view, maybe in full

00:04:09.585 --> 00:04:12.735
text view, listen to it, read it.

00:04:13.365 --> 00:04:17.565
Of course, if I spoke more, I'd get
better at speaking, but let's not

00:04:17.565 --> 00:04:21.345
underestimate the power of listening
and reading because when you are

00:04:21.345 --> 00:04:27.105
reading.,You're interpreting symbols and
converting those symbols into meaning.

00:04:27.135 --> 00:04:29.805
That is a powerful
activity that is active.

00:04:29.925 --> 00:04:30.945
That's not just passive.

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:37.025
Now we know, for example, you've heard
that say a London taxi driver who has

00:04:37.025 --> 00:04:40.755
to memorize, has to get to know all the
little streets and alleyways in london.

00:04:41.545 --> 00:04:45.365
That part of his brain, a certain
part of his brain grows as a result.

00:04:45.635 --> 00:04:50.355
So yeah, all of our activity, even what
we might consider to be passive activity

00:04:50.445 --> 00:04:53.175
in fact, is creating changes in the brain.

00:04:53.445 --> 00:04:55.635
Learning is about change.

00:04:56.055 --> 00:04:58.965
Learning is not about being
able to produce something.

00:04:58.995 --> 00:05:01.575
Now, you know, here's
a test, get it right.

00:05:01.575 --> 00:05:02.325
It's not about that.

00:05:02.655 --> 00:05:07.755
It's about gradually exposing
yourself to a variety of stimulus,

00:05:07.875 --> 00:05:10.305
allowing your brain to form patterns.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.730
And, and having the confidence
that the brain is doing its job.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:19.020
It's not going to do it, you
know, according to some timetable

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:20.220
that you set for the brain.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.100
The brain learns, but
the brain learns slowly.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:24.690
That's what Spitzer says.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:32.130
Similarly, even if you are an adult say,
or an older person like me, and maybe

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.800
you create new neural connections more
slowly, or the plasticity of the brain

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:43.725
is not as quick as it used to be when you
were 12, on the other hand, the brain is

00:05:43.755 --> 00:05:48.135
bringing in this information, relating
it to things that you already know,

00:05:48.345 --> 00:05:53.385
experience, life, experience, knowledge,
vocabulary, uh, you know, knowledge

00:05:53.385 --> 00:05:55.725
about different content areas and stuff.

00:05:56.175 --> 00:05:58.785
All of which makes it easier
in some ways for you to learn.

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:02.655
But the process is essentially
the same and you have to have

00:06:02.655 --> 00:06:05.115
confidence in that ability.

00:06:05.235 --> 00:06:09.975
Once you've had enough exposure to
the language, uh, your brain has

00:06:09.975 --> 00:06:11.475
started to form certain patterns.

00:06:11.715 --> 00:06:15.045
If you want to look at some
rules to remind you of things

00:06:15.045 --> 00:06:17.985
or to point some things out that
you didn't notice, that's fine.

00:06:18.345 --> 00:06:22.695
But the idea that somehow the
brain can absorb specific rules or

00:06:22.695 --> 00:06:26.475
specific lists, that they can, that
the brain can deliberately learn

00:06:26.475 --> 00:06:31.020
these things at one sitting I think
has been increasingly debunked.

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:36.690
So learning to learn means learning
to learn in a way that corresponds to

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:41.070
how your brain learns or how you would
like to think of your brain learning,

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.289
because it's also very subjective.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:49.169
And so putting pressure on yourself to,
uh, you know, to, to learn certain things

00:06:49.169 --> 00:06:53.549
that I want to learn them now, eh, that
is not a very effective way of learning.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.000
If you can find a way to enjoy, in
the case of languages, content, or

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.990
if you're trying to learn through the
language about the history of a certain

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:11.070
period, if you can get a variety of
different content items on a related

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:15.480
theme, where to some extent related
vocabulary is going to appear, and the

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.190
subject that you are gradually becoming
familiar with, you know, shows up in

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:24.600
all of these different stories, uh,
all of that is going to help you learn.

00:07:24.945 --> 00:07:28.424
So, so you have to have this
approach of just letting it come

00:07:28.424 --> 00:07:34.185
to you, having the confidence that
you can do it, um, enjoying the

00:07:34.185 --> 00:07:36.044
process and trusting your brain.

00:07:36.044 --> 00:07:43.724
I think a big part of learning how to
learn is to, to learn to trust your brain.

00:07:44.055 --> 00:07:47.895
And don't worry whether you
got five out of 10 on a drill.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:51.980
Uh, or whether you remembered a
certain, uh, you know, word list.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:57.090
If the, the language comes
into you and you keep staying

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:58.290
with it, you're going to learn.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:07:59.340
That's what you need to know.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.250
That's to me learning how to learn.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:07.200
Don't let the teacher impose
this word list or these

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.630
stories, get out and explore.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.900
Let the brain enjoy the language.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:17.325
Let yourself enjoy the language
and the brain will learn.

00:08:17.955 --> 00:08:21.734
And I think learning how to learn
is, is no more complicated than that.

00:08:21.885 --> 00:08:26.715
Yes, there are details on where you can
go find, you know, content of interest

00:08:26.715 --> 00:08:28.484
and people can help you find that.

00:08:28.755 --> 00:08:32.294
Or how do you, how do you utilize,
you know, Google translate or text

00:08:32.294 --> 00:08:37.334
to speech or Netflix or YouTube or
LingQ or any of the other, you know,

00:08:37.334 --> 00:08:41.595
online dictionaries and different
apps and all of that kind of stuff.

00:08:41.625 --> 00:08:43.775
But those are the details again.

00:08:44.755 --> 00:08:49.785
Learning how to learn to me means
having a, uh, um, sort of, uh, a

00:08:49.785 --> 00:08:56.415
vision of yourself as a learner, which
corresponds to how the brain learns and

00:08:56.415 --> 00:08:58.935
just sit back and enjoy the process.

00:08:58.964 --> 00:09:00.555
And you will succeed.

00:09:00.765 --> 00:09:01.574
You will succeed.

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:03.615
You have to believe that
you're going to succeed.

00:09:03.795 --> 00:09:05.295
Don't second guess yourself.

00:09:05.925 --> 00:09:08.714
And so that's what I wanted to talk about.

00:09:09.225 --> 00:09:13.485
And, um, I'm going to leave you
with two videos that I did, uh,

00:09:14.175 --> 00:09:17.835
Over the past few years, which touch
on the same theme because more or

00:09:17.835 --> 00:09:21.285
less the, the theme is constant.

00:09:21.795 --> 00:09:25.455
You have to believe in yourself
and you have to enjoy the process.

